Pine: good fuel because it lights easily, but not ideal for long-term heavy construction or ship frames.

Oak: one of the best all-round structural woods; excellent for strong buildings, weapons, and traditional shipbuilding.

Cedar: valued more for rot resistance and lightness than brute strength; solid for buildings and some ship uses.

Ash: excellent for shafts, handles, bows, and impact-tolerant weapon parts; less famous than oak for major structural work.

Ebony: very dense and valuable, but usually too rare, heavy, and difficult to work for large-scale practical construction.

Bamboo: very useful in the right culture/tech context, especially for light construction, spears, bows, scaffolding, and fast-growing utility.


